    "content": "The Security Minister of South West State, Osman Isaaq Mohamed, has called on the Kenyan government to arrest former regional president Abdikasis Hassan Mohamed Laftagareen and hand him over to Somali authorities. The request, made on August 23, 2026, follows deadly clashes in Baidoa that officials attribute to forces loyal to the former leader.\n\nMinister Mohamed stated that Laftagareen is accused of orchestrating recent bombings and attacks in Baidoa, the regional capital. He urged Kenya to cooperate through Interpol channels to facilitate Laftagareen's transfer to Somalia for questioning and accountability. The minister's appeal highlights rising tensions between the current administration and the ousted former president.\n\nThe appeal comes after weeks of heavy fighting in Baidoa, where security forces clashed with militias reportedly commanded by Laftagareen's allies. The violence has displaced civilians and disrupted daily life in the city, which has also served as a base for African Union forces. The clashes are part of a broader power struggle that has unsettled the region since Laftagareen's departure from office.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development underscores the fragile security situation in parts of South West State and raises concerns about potential escalating instability that could affect families and investments back home. Diaspora communities often watch such political conflicts closely, as they influence peace efforts and the return of refugees.\n\nThe Kenyan government has not yet responded publicly to the extradition request. Analysts note that Kenya has previously hosted Somali politicians and may tread carefully to maintain diplomatic ties with both Somali federal and regional authorities.",
